The circuits that interface things — driver circuits, level shifters, PWM-as-DAC, the bench layer between software and actuators.

The things Peter makes to interface things: circuit design and construction — driver circuits, level shifters, PWM-as-DAC + RC-filter stages, transistor/MOSFET drivers, sensor front-ends, connectors and power feeds for the actuators he builds by hand. Where a schematic gets drawn, a circuit gets breadboarded, and findings ("beta-limited at ~200 mA") get written down so the next build starts from knowledge, not scratch. It sits *between* [[home-automation]] and [[hardware]]: `hardware` owns the host as a machine, `home-automation` integrates off-the-shelf smart-home kit, and electronics owns the circuits Peter designs and builds himself — the soldering-iron-and-oscilloscope layer neither of the other two covers. It owns *how* a circuit is built; the driven project owns *why*.
